# Skrivro config file: every key with its compiled-in default value,
# all commented out. This file ships with the source tree as a
# reference. To customize Skrivro, copy it to the platform-appropriate
# location:
#
#   Linux:   $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/com.skrivro.editor/skrivro.conf
#            (or ~/.config/com.skrivro.editor/skrivro.conf if unset)
#   macOS:   ~/Library/Application Support/com.skrivro.editor/skrivro.conf
#   Windows: %APPDATA%\com.skrivro.editor\skrivro.conf
#
# then uncomment any line and edit. Every value shown below is the
# actual compiled-in default. Uncommenting a line without changing
# it is a no-op.
#
# User-supplied themes live alongside this file in a themes/ subdir,
# e.g., Linux: ~/.config/com.skrivro.editor/themes/<name>.theme
# A user-supplied theme with the same name as a bundled theme overrides
# the bundled version.
#
# Skrivro's parser ignores missing keys, malformed lines, and unknown
# keys (with debug-mode warnings only), so you can leave whatever you
# don't care about commented out. Edits apply on next restart. There
# is no hot reload.
#
# Duplicate keys: last declaration wins. If you end up with two
# uncommented lines for the same key (e.g., two `theme = ...` lines
# left over while trying out different themes), the lower one takes
# effect. The parser iterates top-to-bottom and each assignment
# overwrites the previous. Same behavior as most flat config formats.

# ================= Fonts =================
# Font family keys are PREPENDED to the built-in CSS font stack, so
# if the font you pick isn't installed on the system the renderer
# falls through to the original stack. Your setting is a preference,
# not a hard requirement.
#
# ALL length-typed values (font sizes, padding) REQUIRE an explicit
# CSS unit. Bare numbers are rejected: writing `14` for a font size
# is invalid. Write `14pt` (or `14px`, `1rem`, etc.). The CSS `pt`
# unit is the same as the typographic point used by Word,
# PowerPoint, and other print-oriented tools: 1/72 inch. Zero and
# negative values are also rejected.

# Editor (monospace) font. Built-in fallback stack:
#   ui-monospace, JetBrains Mono, Cascadia Code, Fira Code,
#   Menlo, Monaco, Consolas, monospace
# edit-font = ui-monospace

# Preview (sans-serif) font. Built-in fallback stack:
#   -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, Inter, Segoe UI, Roboto,
#   Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif
# preview-font = system-ui

# edit-font-size = 0.95rem
# preview-font-size = 1rem

# ================= Layout =================
# Padding keys are split by axis: `-x` for horizontal (left/right),
# `-y` for vertical (top/bottom). Each key accepts one or two values.
# One value applies uniformly to both ends of the axis. Two values
# are in reading order: for `-y` that's `top bottom`, for `-x` that's
# `left right`. Every token requires an explicit unit (same rule as
# font sizes). Three or more values are rejected.
#
# Example (asymmetric top vs. bottom):
#   editor-padding-y = 2rem 3rem     # top = 2rem, bottom = 3rem
#
# Example (uniform horizontal):
#   editor-padding-x = 20px          # both left and right = 20px
#
# Vertical padding scrollbar-length caveat: both panes clip their
# scrollable viewport to sit inside the padding (geometric clipping
# instead of an overlay: the overlay approach exhibits sub-pixel
# bleed on fractional-DPI displays that cannot be fixed in CSS).
# That means the scroll container is shorter than the pane by
# 2 × padding-y, and the native scrollbar track shrinks accordingly.
# At the default 2rem, this is barely noticeable. At values around
# 6rem or more, the scrollbar track becomes visibly stubby on
# typical-height viewports. Large values are accepted (config is
# your reading comfort), just be aware of the shortening.

# editor-padding-x = 2.5rem
# editor-padding-y = 2rem
# preview-padding-x = 2.5rem
# preview-padding-y = 2rem

# ================= Theme =================
# Bundled themes:
#   Dark:  catppuccin-mocha (default), dracula, gruvbox-dark, nord,
#          tokyo-night-moon
#   Light: catppuccin-latte, tokyo-night-day
#
# Set `theme = dracula` (or any other name) to switch. Omit the key
# (or set it to catppuccin-mocha) for the default.
#
# User-supplied themes: place a .theme file in the themes/ subdir
# alongside this config file:
#   Linux:   ~/.config/com.skrivro.editor/themes/<name>.theme
#   macOS:   ~/Library/Application Support/com.skrivro.editor/themes/<name>.theme
#   Windows: %APPDATA%\com.skrivro.editor\themes\<name>.theme
# then set `theme = <name>` here. A user-supplied file with the same
# name as a bundled theme overrides the bundled version. The bundled
# templates in the source tree end in `.theme.default` to signal
# "reference template, not a live config". The user file is the same
# format with the `.default` suffix dropped. Copy
# resources/themes/catppuccin-mocha.theme.default to
# <app_config_dir>/themes/<name>.theme and fill in your palette.
# All 24 slots are documented inline in the template.
# theme = catppuccin-mocha

# ================= Format =================

# Default format assigned to a blank untitled buffer: asciidoc /
# markdown / text (default asciidoc)
#
# Applies only when a launch has no file to open: no CLI argument,
# no OS-dispatched file-open event, and either no saved session to
# restore or an empty one. File-extension detection always wins:
# opening foo.md is markdown regardless of what this key says.
# Saving an untitled buffer with a specific extension (via :saveas
# foo.md or the save-as dialog) switches it accordingly.
#
# 'text' is anything that isn't AsciiDoc or GitHub-flavored Markdown:
# loose notes, untitled-without-extension scratchpads, or files
# like .txt / .cpp / .js / .css / .html that aren't a supported
# markup format. Intended for users whose primary habit is writing
# plain prose without formatting.
#
# Unknown values (e.g. `default-format = pandoc`) are rejected with
# a debug warning and fall through to asciidoc.
# default-format = asciidoc

# ================= Asciidoctor =================
# Asciidoctor safe mode: unsafe / safe / server / secure
#
# Controls which Asciidoctor features are enabled. `unsafe` is the
# most permissive: includes across directories, docinfo files, book
# doctype, and arbitrary attribute setting all work. `secure` disables
# nearly all of it. This is a set-and-forget knob, deliberately not
# exposed in the UI. Once you've chosen a safe mode, runtime display
# has no value.
# asciidoc-safe-mode = unsafe

# ================= Status bar =================

# Cursor position format: verbose / compact / ruler
#   verbose: Ln 42, Col 85    (prose-editor default)
#   compact: 42:85            (code-editor style)
#   ruler:   42,85            (Vim ruler style)
# cursor-position-format = verbose

# Status bar mode pill style: canonical / muted
# canonical from lualine, muted from LazyVim, both using the active
# theme's colors rather than a fixed palette
#   canonical: each vim mode gets its own bright accent color
#   muted:     Normal and Visual are toned down, Insert / Replace /
#              Command keep their canonical accents
# statusbar-style = canonical

# Soft column limit: positive integer, no default. When set, the Col
# indicator in the status bar turns peach once the cursor's column
# on the current LOGICAL line exceeds this value. Useful for prose
# workflows with hard line-width conventions (80- or 100-char commit
# messages, reStructuredText, Markdown with strict line limits, etc.).
#
# Character-based: counts actual characters (codepoints), not bytes
# or visual cells. Em-dashes, emoji, and accented letters count as 1.
#
# Document-column, not visual-column: soft wrap is a display concern
# and does NOT reset the count. If you type 150 characters on one
# logical line in a window narrow enough to wrap at 60 characters,
# the indicator still flags at col > limit, because the count is
# based on the source document, not the rendered layout.
#
# No default: omit this key entirely if you write prose as "one
# paragraph per line" (where soft wrap handles the visual display
# and you don't care about source-line length). Set it explicitly
# if your workflow treats source lines as having a fixed width.
# soft-column-limit = 100

# ================= Session =================

# Restore last session on launch: true / false (default true)
#
# When true, launching Skrivro without a CLI argument reopens the
# file that was open at the time of the previous clean exit. The
# file path is persisted in a platform-appropriate state file:
#
#   Linux:   ~/.local/share/com.skrivro.editor/state.json
#   macOS:   ~/Library/Application Support/com.skrivro.editor/state.json
#   Windows: %LOCALAPPDATA%\com.skrivro.editor\state.json
#
# Precedence: a CLI argument (skrivro foo.adoc) always wins over
# session restore. Crash recovery via the autosave draft also takes
# priority. If you were mid-edit when the app died, you get the
# draft restored (not the pre-crash file reloaded from disk). Session
# restore only fires on a clean launch with no CLI arg and no draft.
#
# If the restored path is missing or unreadable, Skrivro silently
# falls back to a blank buffer (no error dialog), but the next file
# operation updates the state as normal.
#
# Behavior note: the state file is updated on every file operation
# (open, save-as, new, :e filename) regardless of this setting, so
# session state is always tracked. The setting only controls whether
# that state is READ at launch. Because it defaults to on, a clean
# launch with no file argument reopens the most recently touched
# file. Set this to false if you would rather always start with a
# blank buffer. To keep restore on but start fresh once, delete the
# state file before the next launch (see path above for your
# platform).
#
# Accepted forms: true/false, yes/no, on/off, 1/0 (case-insensitive).
# restore-session = true

# ================= Security =================

# Allow external HTTPS image loading in the preview pane: true /
# false (default true)
#
# When true (the default), the gate permits HTTPS image src values
# through. The webview loads them normally via Tauri's CSP-allowed
# https: source. Useful for previewing GitHub-hosted READMEs and
# similar documents where images live on external CDNs.
#
# When false, every `<img>` whose src is an external URL (anything
# other than `data:` or Tauri's `asset:` protocol) is replaced at
# render time with a small inline placeholder showing the alt text
# and the original URL on hover. No HTTP request is ever sent. The
# gate runs in the frontend BEFORE the rendered HTML enters the live
# DOM, so the webview never sees an external image src to fetch.
#
# HTTPS-only policy: even when this flag is true, plain `http:`
# image sources are still placeholdered. There's no opt-in for HTTP.
#
# Privacy implication: with this on, every preview render of a
# document with external images sends your IP and User-Agent to
# whatever servers host those images (a tracking vector for
# documents from untrusted sources). Set this to false if you render
# documents you do not trust.
#
# CSP and restart: this flag does not require Tauri's CSP to be
# modified. The CSP already allows `https:` for img-src. The gate is
# what blocks when this is off. So toggling this and restarting
# Skrivro is sufficient. (No Skrivro setting can override Tauri's CSP
# at runtime. If you ever need to lock down the CSP further, that's a
# tauri.conf.json edit + rebuild.)
#
# Accepted forms: true/false, yes/no, on/off, 1/0 (case-insensitive).
# allow-external-images = true

# ================= Language =================

# UI language for the status bar labels, help dialog, and confirm
# dialogs: auto / en / sv. Default auto (detect from browser/system
# locale: sv when it's Swedish, en otherwise).
#
# This localizes the user-facing text in Skrivro's own chrome only.
# It does NOT translate:
#   - Vim Ex commands (:w, :open, :saveas, :syncpreview, etc.): these
#     stay English because they're input syntax, same rule real Vim
#     follows regardless of locale
#   - Keyboard shortcut names (Ctrl+S on Linux/Windows, ⌃⌥T on Mac):
#     physical key identifiers, not translatable text
#   - Your document content or AsciiDoc rendered output: Skrivro
#     doesn't touch document text at all
#
# Supported values:
#   auto: detect from navigator.language (sv* → Swedish, everything
#         else → English). Default if this key is unset.
#   en:   force English regardless of system locale
#   sv:   force Swedish regardless of system locale
#
# Unknown values (e.g. `language = de`) are rejected with a debug
# warning and fall through to auto-detect. If you want a language
# other than English or Swedish, there's no translation for it yet:
# the translation table lives in src/i18n.ts's STRINGS_SV
# and is easy to extend if someone contributes a translation.
# language = auto

# ================= Spellcheck =================

# Offline editor spellcheck: auto / off / en / sv / both (default auto)
#
# When a language is active, Skrivro underlines misspelled words in the
# editor with a red squiggle, using a Hunspell dictionary. The checking
# is entirely offline and local: no network, no service. US English is
# built in. Swedish and other variants are supplied by you (see the
# Dictionaries note below).
#
#   auto: the default. Detect the dictionary from your system locale
#         (a Swedish locale gets Swedish, everything else gets English)
#         and turn spellcheck on. It reads the locale directly, so it is
#         independent of the `language` key above: an English UI with
#         auto still spellchecks in Swedish on a Swedish system.
#   off:  disabled. No dictionary is loaded. A HARD off: the runtime
#         toggle has no effect while this is off (there is nothing to
#         toggle).
#   en:   English. The built-in dictionary is US English. For another
#         variant (UK, Canadian, Australian), drop your own en.dic and
#         en.aff in the dictionaries folder (see below).
#   sv:   Swedish. Not built in, so you supply sv.dic and sv.aff (see
#         below). Without them, Swedish spellcheck stays off and the
#         status bar says so.
#   both: English and Swedish at once. A word is accepted if either
#         dictionary knows it. Useful for mixed-language documents. With
#         no Swedish files in place it checks English only.
#
# This is separate from the `language` key above: that sets the UI
# chrome language. This sets which spelling dictionary checks your
# prose. They're independent. You can have an English UI and a Swedish
# spellchecker, or vice versa.
#
# Runtime toggle: when spellcheck is on (auto or an explicit language),
# you can silence or restore the squiggles at runtime without a restart
# (the keybind is in Help). That on/off state is remembered across
# launches. The language here is what gets loaded.
#
# Dictionaries: only US English ships with the app. To add Swedish or a
# different English variant, put a Hunspell pair in a dictionaries folder
# inside your Skrivro config directory (where skrivro.conf lives): sv.dic
# and sv.aff for Swedish, or en.dic and en.aff in any English variant to
# take the place of the built-in US English. You can have both pairs in
# place at once, not just one or the other. CONFIG.adoc in the project
# repository has the exact download links and supported variants.
#
# Unknown values are rejected with a debug warning and fall through to
# auto (the default).
# spellcheck-language = auto
